% ============================================================================
% reference-line-hemisphere-location — the SINGLE hemisphere a source states
% a tropic sits within, decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside
% `reference-line-degree.adj`'s own already-quoted NOAA NESDIS source
% sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `reference-line-degree.adj`
% (`reference_line_degree(line, degrees)`, tropic_of_cancer → 23.5,
% tropic_of_capricorn → -23.5). That table's own already-quoted NOAA NESDIS
% span ALSO names, in plain words, which single hemisphere each tropic sits
% within -- a fact the numeric-degree schema had no room for:
%
%     "One in the Northern Hemisphere called the Tropic of Cancer at +23.5°
%      latitude and one in the Southern Hemisphere called the Tropic of
%      Capricorn at − 23.5° latitude."
%
% New `reference_line_hemisphere_location(line, hemisphere)` table decodes
% that span as its own rows: tropic_of_cancer → northern, tropic_of_capricorn
% → southern.
%
% THIS IS NOT A DUPLICATE of the already-shipped
% `reference_line_hemisphere_split(line, hemispheres)` table. That table
% answers a DIFFERENT question -- "which PAIR of hemispheres does this line
% DIVIDE the Earth into" -- and only grounds for equator and prime_meridian,
% the two lines that pass through 0° and split the globe in half. This table
% answers "which SINGLE hemisphere does this line SIT WITHIN" -- and only
% grounds for the two tropics, which sit entirely inside one hemisphere each
% (unlike the equator/prime_meridian, a tropic does not divide the Earth into
% two named halves). The two tables are disjoint over lines (hemisphere_split
% covers equator/prime_meridian; hemisphere_location covers the tropics) and
% disjoint in what they assert (a two-way split vs. a single containing
% hemisphere), so there is no overlap in purpose or rows.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? reference_line_hemisphere_location(tropic_of_cancer, $Hemisphere)   % northern
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `reference_line_hemisphere_location(line, hemisphere)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the
% engine returns the hemisphere WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of these two tropics (it
% never invents a hemisphere). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a
% hemisphere and recall which tropic it names:
%
%     ? reference_line_hemisphere_location($Line, southern)   % tropic_of_capricorn
%
% A recall on `equator` or `prime_meridian` -- real, already-tabled reference
% lines, but ones whose OWN cited span never names a single containing
% hemisphere for them (their own facts are about DIVIDING the Earth, already
% captured in `reference_line_hemisphere_split`) -- correctly abstains here
% rather than inventing an answer. A recall on `arctic_circle` or
% `antarctic_circle` -- real, already-tabled lines whose own quoted span
% never names a hemisphere at all (only "around the North/South Pole") --
% also correctly abstains.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sentence already quoted
% inside `reference-line-degree.adj`'s own header, from the SAME NOAA NESDIS
% "What Is a Solstice?" page that table already cites -- no new WebFetch,
% only a different schema decoding a different clause of that
% already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (NOAA NESDIS is a primary U.S. government reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table reference_line_hemisphere_location {
    columns line, hemisphere

    row (tropic_of_cancer, northern)
    row (tropic_of_capricorn, southern)

    source "One in the Northern Hemisphere called the Tropic of Cancer at +23.5° latitude and one in the Southern Hemisphere called the Tropic of Capricorn at − 23.5° latitude."
    locator "https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/optical-phenomena/what-solstice"
    trust authoritative
}
